## Comprehensive Summary
The environmental contamination of uranium will occur in scenarios such as nuclear accidents and leakage from nuclear waste storage sites, which eventually leads to the internal uranium exposure of people, causing consequential injuries of renal failure, osteosarcoma, etc. The development of uranyl specific chelating agents that could sequester uranium in vivo is in urgent need and is important for the safe and efficient development of nuclear industry. Metal organic frameworks (MOFs) already serve as efficient uranium depletion materials in solutions of a wide range of pH and ionic strength for nuclear fuel recycling, uranium extraction from seawater, as well as environmental decontamination. Herein, a chromium-based nano-metal organic framework (nano-MOF) functionalized interiorly with 3,2-HOPO ligands, MIL-101-HOPO, is rationally synthesized. In vitro adsorption experiments show that MIL-101-HOPO exhibits high adsorption selectivity and fast adsorption kinetics for uranyl. The results of in vivo uranyl decorporation assays reveal that MIL-101-HOPO with the decoration of HOPO ligands on the interior wall exhibits significantly increased uranyl removal ratio in kidneys comparing to the pristine nMOFs, and is more effective than the clinically used $Z n N a_{3}$ -DTPA. All those results corroborate the interior functionalization of MOFs as an efficient strategy to develop promising uranyl decorporation agents.
Keywords
Metal-organic frameworks | Uranium | 3,2-HOPO | Adsorption | Sequestration
## Background and Originality Content
As the major source of nuclear fuel, uranium is utilized in vast amount. However, the accidental failure of nuclear power plants and the broken of nuclear waste containers in storage sites may release uranium into the environment, which will then enter the human body through the respiratory tract, digestive tract, or wound-related absorption.[1-3] Though a fraction of uranium is readily excreted via urine, the remaining uranium mainly deposits in kidneys and bones as uranyl ions, $\mathsf{U O}_{2}^{2+}$ . The formation of stable complexes of uranyl with proteins and hydroxyapatite renders the excretion of uranium difficult.[4-5] The radiotoxicity study shows that the primary injury of uranyl is renal dysfunction, followed by liver damage and osteosarcoma.[6-7]
To promote the uranyl excretion, the key is to develop a chelation agent, which can outcompete proteins and hydroxyapatite in forming complexes with uranyl in the presence of endogenous divalent cations in vivo. Unfortunately, there is still lack of effective uranyl decorporation agents. The effect of clinical agents, diethylenetriamine pentaacetate sodium salts $\boldsymbol{[Z n N a_{3}}$ -DTPA and $\mathsf{C a N a}_{3}$ -DTPA) and sodium bicarbonate, is extremely limited in uranyl removal. Besides, their toxicity also restrains the extensive and repeated usage clinically.[5,8-9] Hydroxypyridone (HOPO) ligands have been considered as the optimal actinide decorporation ligands in the past decades,[7,10] and the highest uranyl removal ratio in bone was reported using a novel tetradentate hydroxypyridone chelator, 5LIO-1-Cm-3,2-HOPO, by our group.[11]
To overcome the disadvantages of fast excretion, relative high toxicity, and poor uranium decorporation efficacy of molecular agents, functionalized nanomaterials, such as nano-metal organic frameworks,[12] chitosan nanoparticles,[13] melanin nanoparticles,[14] and covalent organic nanosheets,[15] have been reported by our group. The exterior functionalization of ligands would diminish the removal efficacy of uranyl or ROS as observed in the cases of chitosan nanoparticles decorated with HOPO ligands on the surface (COS-HOPO) and melanin nanoparticles with attached PEG (MNPs-PEG).[13-14] The porous materials have been widely used in the uranium extraction and decontamination.[16-17] Functional metal organic frameworks (MOFs) can effectively and quickly adsorb uranyl into the channels with high structural stability even in aqua regia,[18-19] and functional covalent organic frameworks (COFs) can successfully extract uranium from seawater and alkaline solutions.[20-21] Besides, the in vivo detoxification of salicylic acid and uremic toxins has been achieved using MIL-127 and NU-1000, respectively, demonstrating high biocompatibility and low toxicity of those materials.[22-23] Thereafter, we synthesized a carboxyl-functionalized nMOFs (UiO-66- $(C O O H)_{4}$ - 180), in which carboxyl groups are pre-organized in the pores to improve the selectivity and binding ability of uranyl.[12] As a result, UiO-66- $(C O O H)_{4}-180$ exhibits a uranyl sequestration ratio that is three times higher than that of the pentacarboxylate ligand $Z n N a_{3}$ -DTPA in vivo. The CON-AO obtained via the attachment of AO groups to the channels of covalent organic sheets also exhibits elevated decorporation efficacy of uranyl than DTPA ligands. Both cases demonstrate the superiority of interior functionalization of metal chelating units in improving the uranyl removal ability of the porous material.
However, carboxylate ligands such as ethylene diamine tetraacetic acid (EDTA) and DTPA suffer from poor uranium binding selectivity, while the binding affinity of amidoxime ligands towards uranyl is not as strong as carboxylate ligands. Moreover, the small channel size of UiO-66 might hinder the uptake of uranyl. In comparison, MIL-101 has unique mesoporous cages $(29\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ and $34\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ in diameter) with large pore windows $.12\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ of the pentagonal widow and $16\mathring{\mathsf{A}}\times14.5\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ of the hexagonal window).[24-25] Owing to its large drug loading aperture and tunable drug-pore interactions, MIL-101 has been widely used in biomedical applications, such as biocatalysis,[26] bioimaging,[27] and drug delivery.[28-31] Furthermore, after activation and dehydration at high temperature, lots of unsaturated coordination sites are exposed at the metal centers of MIL-101, which can be combined with electron-rich groups.[32-33] For instance, MIL-101 can be readily functionalized with various ligands such as diethylenetriamine (DETA), octyl(phenyl)-N,N-diisobutylcarbamoylmethylphosphine oxide (CMPO), and diaminomaleonitrile (DAMN) via postsynthetic methods. The as-synthesized MIL-101-DETA, MIL-101-CMPO, and double-shelled hollow (DSHM) MIL-101-DAMN show excellent uranium adsorption performance,[34-36] among which the saturation capacity of MIL-101(DSHM)-DAMN is up to $601\mathrm{mg/g},$ over 30 times higher than that of the raw materials. A rough estimation shows that even after the attachment of the optimal HOPO ligands (ca. 11.5 Å for two ligands), the pores in MIL-101 still provide sufficient room to accommodate $\mathsf{U O}_{2}^{2+}$ which is $6.04-6.84\mathrm{~\AA~}$ in length.[37-39] Taking the aforementioned advantages of MIL-101 and HOPO ligands into consideration, we propose using HOPO functionalized MIL-101 for the decorporation of uranium in vivo.
Herein, a novel HOPO-functionalized nMOF, MIL-101-HOPO, was designed and synthesized by attaching the optimal actinide chelating ligand 3,2-HOPO into the nanoscale MIL-101. Results of powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD), $^1\mathsf{H}$ nuclear magnetic resonance (NMR) analysis, Fourier transform infrared (FT-IR) spectroscopy, transmission electron microscope (TEM), and dynamic light scattering (DLS) reveal the successful synthesis of the nanoscale MIL-101-HOPO. The uranyl removal capability of MIL-101 materials was evaluated by adsorption experiments and in vivo uranyl decorporation assays, demonstrating that the functionalization of HOPO ligands in MIL-101 results in more efficient and rapid removal of uranyl comparing to the pristine $M\mathsf{I L}\mathsf{-}\mathsf{101-N H}_{2}$ .
## Results and Discussion
## Synthesis and characterization
The synthesis of the HOPO-functionalized nano-MOF, MIL-101-HOPO, was carried out by grafting HOPO groups into MIL-101 (Figure S1). Specifically, amino-functionalized MIL-101 $(M l-101-N H_{2})$ was synthesized first and then decorated with carboxyl-functionalized 3-hydroxy-2-pyridinone (3,2-HOPO-COOH) by subjecting the amino and carboxyl groups to amidation reactions. PXRD data of MIL-101- $\cdot N H_{2}$ and MIL-101-HOPO were collected and compared with that of the simulated MIL-101-type skeleton in the database. As shown in Figure 1a, the perfect agreement between the experimental and theoretical peak positions confirms the formation of the desired structure. FT-IR spectroscopy and $^1\mathsf{H}$ NMR analysis were performed to identify the functional groups of the samples (Figures 1b and S2a). Firstly, FT-IR spectrum of 3,2-HOPO-COOH shows broad absorption in $3500{-}2000~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , which is attributed to the stretching vibration of $-C H_{2},$ aromatic $-C\mathsf{H},$ , and $-\mathsf{O H}$ groups, and the peaks at 1699 $\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ and $1646~{\mathsf{c m}}^{-1}$ are in the region for the $-C=0$ stretching vibrations.[40] Combined with the $^1\mathsf{H}$ NMR data $\binom{1}{\mathsf{H}}$ NMR $(400\mathsf{M H z},$ DMSO- $\cdot d_{6.}$ ) δ: 12.95 (s, 1H), 9.04 (s, 1H), 7.11 (dd, $\ J=6.8$ , $\mathsf{1.8~H z},$ 1H), 6.70 (dd, $J=7.2$ , $1.7\ \mathsf{H z}$ , 1H), 6.08 (t, $J=7.0~{\mathsf{H z}}$ , 1H), 4.62 (s, 2H)), the formation of 3,2-HOPO-COOH ligand with high purity is revealed (Figure S2a). The FT-IR peaks at 3474 and $3367~\mathrm{{cm}}^{-1}$ are attributed to the asymmetric and symmetric stretching vibrations of $-N H_{2},$ while the peak at $1,622~{\mathsf{c m}}^{-1}$ is ascribed to the ${\mathsf{N}}{-}{\mathsf{H}}$ bending vibration and the peak at $1,258~{\mathsf{c m}}^{-1}$ is attributed to the C—N stretching vibration of the pristine MOF material.[34-36,41-42]
Furthermore, the morphology and particle sizes of the materials were characterized by TEM and DLS (Figures 1c and 1d). The calculated average diameters of $M\mathsf{I L}\mathsf{-}\mathsf{101-N H}_{2}$ and MIL-101-HOPO particles are only $103\ \mathsf{n m}$ $\mathsf{P D I}=0.393\mathrm{\mathrm{;}}$ ) and $162\mathsf{n m}$ ( $\mathsf{P D I}=0.634)$ , respectively, which are within the suitable size range for decorporation materials.[43]
## In vitro adsorption experiments
In vitro adsorption experiments were carried out to assess adsorption kinetics and adsorption selectivity of MIL-101-HOPO. The adsorption kinetics assays of $\mathsf{U}(\mathsf{V}|)$ by $M\mathsf{I L}\mathsf{-}\mathsf{101-N H}_{2}$ and MIL-101-HOPO were first conducted (Figure 2a). The uptake of uranyl from the aqueous solution $(2.0~\mathrm{mg/L}~\mathsf{U}(\mathsf{V}|).$ , $\mathsf{p H}=5.5$ ) by MIL-101-HOPO was extremely fast, reaching adsorption equilibrium within 10 min and removing $74\%$ of uranyl. In sharp contrast, $M\mathsf{I L}\mathsf{-}\mathsf{101-N H}_{2}$ did not reach adsorption equilibrium until $180~\mathrm{{min}}$ , with a final removal rate of only $34\%$ . This can be ascribed to the modification of HOPO functional groups, which brings in more binding sites for uranyl that is beneficial for the adsorption capacity and kinetics.[44] Moreover, the experimental data were fitted by pseudo-first-order model and pseudo-second-order model (Table S1). According to Figure 2b and Figure S2b, the adsorption kinetics of MIL-101-HOPO is consistent with pseudo-second-order model, indicating that the adsorption of MIL-101-HOPO toward U(VI) is predominantly chemisorption, i.e., the complexation with uranyl.[45-46] The adsorption model of $M\mathsf{I L}\mathsf{-}\mathsf{101-N H}_{2}$ also consists with chemisorption (Figure S2c and S2d). The PXRD peaks of the MOF samples after adsorption remain intact (Figure 2c), indicating the preservation of mesoporous cages after the uptake of uranyl by MIL-101-HOPO.
The depletion of endogenous divalent ions is a major cause of the toxicity of the decorporation ligands, to avoid which a high binding selectivity of uranyl is critical. Therefore, competing adsorption experiments were conducted in HEPES buffer solution $\left(\mathsf{p H}=7.4\right)$ ) containing $4.0~\mathrm{mg/L}$ of U(VI) and $10.0~\mathsf{m g/L}$ of Ca(II), Mg(II), Zn(II), Mn(II), Co(II), and Ni(II) to investigate the adsorption selectivity of MIL-101-HOPO toward uranyl. The experimental results are illustrated in Figure 2d. When the concentration of competing endogenous metal ions is 2.5 times that of uranyl, MIL-101-HOPO can still adsorb $86\%$ of uranyl but only ca. $20\%$ of $\mathsf{M n}(\mathsf{I I})$ , less than $10\%$ of Ca(II), $Z\mathsf{n}(11),\mathsf{C o}({\mathsf{I I}}),\mathsf{N i}({\mathsf{I I}}).$ , and ca. $0.04\%$ of Mg(II). The competitive adsorption experiments demonstrate the high selectivity and affinity of MIL-101-HOPO and MIL-101- $\cdot\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ toward uranyl under physiological conditions. Besides, the higher adsorption efficiency at ${\mathsf{p H}}7.4$ than at ${\mathsf{p H}}5.5$ could be ascribed to the higher deprotonation of the chelating moieties at higher pH, as reported for many porous adsorbents including MIL-101 materials.[34]
## Cell cytotoxicity assays
CCK-8 kit assays were performed to assess the combined cytotoxicity of uranium and chelating agents. Given the preferred accumulation of uranyl in kidneys, rat proximal renal tubular epithelial cells (NRK-52E) were used as the experimental model. As previously reported, the uranyl concentration of $12.4\mathrm{~.}\upmu\mathrm{mol/L}$ is considered to be suitable for cytotoxicity evaluation.[13] Hence, the cells were incubated with $12.4~\upmu\mathrm{mol/L}$ of U(VI) and different concentrations of chelating ligands. The results are provided in Figure 3a and Table S2, showing a dosage dependent cell viability for all ligands. At the ligand concentration of 3.125 and 6.25 $\upmu\mathrm{g}/\mathsf{m L}$ , the cell survival rates of MIL-101-HOPO and $M\mathsf{I L}\mathsf{-}\mathsf{101-N H}_{2}$ treated groups were both above $80\%$ , which were slightly lower than that of $Z n N a_{3}$ -DTPA but on par with that of 3,2-HOPO-COOH. Although the cell viability decreased substantially at higher dosage in all treated group, MIL-101-HOPO exhibited much lower cytotoxicity than $\mathsf{M l L}\mathsf{-}101\mathsf{-}\mathsf{N}\mathsf{H}_{2},$ , which is closely related to the higher removal ratio of uranyl and the replacement of amino groups, since the amine-containing polymers are reported to be able to interact with lipid bilayers of cell membranes, causing cell lysis and death.[47] Therefore, the attachment of HOPO ligands simultaneously improves the metal binding ability and reduces the comprehensive toxicity of MIL-101-HOPO comparing to $M\mathsf{I L}\mathsf{-}\mathsf{101-N H}_{2}$ .
## In vivo uranium decorporation assays
On the basis of the superior in vitro adsorption performance of MIL-101-HOPO, in vivo uranium decorporation experiments in mice were conducted. $Z n N a_{3}$ -DTPA, 3,2-HOPO-COOH, MIL-101- $\cdot N H_{2},$ and MIL-101-HOPO were intravenously injected $^\textrm{\scriptsize1h}$ before the U(VI) injection. The results show that $24\ h$ after the initial U(VI) injection, uranium contents in kidneys of mice treated with physiological saline, $Z n N a_{3}$ -DTPA, 3,2-HOPO-COOH, ${\mathsf{M l L}}{\mathsf{-101-N H}}_{2},$ and MIL-101-HOPO were $7.94\pm1.68$ , $7.41\pm1.66$ , $8.28\pm2.51$ , $8.30\pm$ 2.17, and $6.42\pm0.75{\upmu\mathrm{g}}$ per gram tissues, respectively (Figure 3b and Table S3). The kidney uranium content of the MIL-101-HOPO treated group was reduced by $19.1\%$ comparing to the control group, which is the highest among all experimental groups. While the uranium content in kidneys for the $Z n N a_{3}$ -DTPA group only shows a reduction of $6.68\%$ comparing to the control group. In sharp contrast, the uranium content in $M\mathsf{I L}\mathsf{-}\mathsf{101-N H}_{2}$ group was slightly increased by $4.51\%$ , indicating the poor uranyl sequestration in vivo. The decorporation efficacy of HOPO ligands relies heavily on the administration time and method,[48] and the increase in kidney uranium content by the prophylactic administration of HOPO ligand may be related to the non-reversible binding between HOPO and kidneys as reported.[7] In comparison, the HOPO functionalized nano-MOF materials exhibit higher decorporation performance in prophylactic administration than 3,2-HOPO-COOH ligand, which could possibly be attributed to the accumulation of nMOFs in kidneys similar to the UiO-66-COOH nano agents.[12,49] The results of in vivo decorporation experiment demonstrate the interior functionalization is an effective strategy to improve the actinide sequestration ability of nano porous materials. It is noteworthy that the decorporation ratio of MIL-101-HOPO is not as high as that of UiO-66-COOH, despite its larger pore size. Given the high uranyl adsorption selectivity and capacity of other functionalized MIL-101 materials, such as MIL-101(DSHM)-DAMN with a saturation capacity of $601\mathrm{mg/g},$ it is still highly possible to obtain materials with better decorporation performance.
## Conclusions
In summary, this work reveals the advantage of hydroxypyridone functionalized nano-metal organic framework material in decorporating uranium in vivo. The post-modification of HOPO into MIL-101- $\cdot\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ not only improves the adsorption kinetics and capacity of uranyl, but also reduces the comprehensive cytotoxicity comparing to the pristine nMOFs. More importantly, the higher decorporation ratio of uranyl by MIL-101-HOPO than ${\mathsf{M l L}}{\mathsf{-101-N H}}_{2},$ $Z n N a_{3}$ -DTPA, and 3,2-HOPO-COOH demonstrates that the additional HOPO ligands lead to effective improvement of uranium sequestration when administered prophylactically. This work and previous reports corroborate the interior functionalization of porous material is an effective method to improve the performance of nano decorporation agents. Given the easy modification and functionalization nature of nano-MOFs, the attachment of various ligands would probably endow the materials with simultaneous decorporation, imaging, and radiation protection effects, which is beneficial for the nuclear emergency treatment of people.
## Experimental
## Materials and reagents
Caution! Due to the radioactivity of $^{238}\mathsf{U O}_{2}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O},$ all operations should be carried out in accordance with standard precautions for handling radioactive materials. $C r(N O_{3})_{3}{\cdot}9H_{2}O$ $(99\%$ , Macklin), 2-aminoterephthalic acid $(98\%$ Rhawn), 2,3-dihydroxypyridine $(98\%$ Adamas), ethyl bromoacetate $(99\%$ Adamas), NaOH $(96\%,$ , Enox), $N,N\mathrm{.}$ -dimethylformamide (DMF) $(99\%$ Enox), ethanol $199\%$ , Enox), methanol $(99\%,$ , Enox), and 1-(3-dimethylaminopropyl)-3-ethylcarbodiimide hydrochloride (EDC·HCl) $(99\%,$ Aladdin) were all used without further purification in the experiments.
## Material synthesis
Synthesis of 3,2-HOPO-COOH. 3,2-HOPO-COOH was synthesized according to our previously reported procedure (Figure S1).[13] Typically, excess quantities of ethyl bromoacetate were first used to alkylate 2,3-dihydroxypyridine at the N site of the ring at $150~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}.$ . The $-\mathsf{O}\mathsf{H}$ group of the preceding product was then protected with a benzyl moiety, followed by hydrolyzation to obtain the carboxylic group. Subsequently, after adding $5\%\ \mathsf{P d/C}$ and stirring the solution under ${\sf H}_{2}$ atmosphere at room temperature overnight, the benzyl moiety was removed. Finally, the orange solid of 3,2-HOPO-COOH was obtained by rotary evaporation.
Synthesis of MIL-101- $\left.\mathsf{N H}_{2}\right.$ . MIL-101- $N{\sf H}_{2}$ was synthesized according to the published procedure (Figure S1).[50] Typically, $C r(N O_{3})_{3}{\cdot}9H_{2}O$ $(400~\mathsf{m g})$ , 2-aminoterephthalic acid $(181~\mathsf{m g})$ , and NaOH $(80~\mathsf{m g})$ were mixed in DI water $(7.5~\mathsf{m L})$ with a molar ratio of $1:1:2$ and sonicated for 15 min. The resulting solution was moved into a $20~\mathsf{m L}$ Teflon-lined stainless-steel autoclave and heated at $150^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $12\mathsf{h}$ . The reaction was then cooled to room temperature and centrifuged at $11000~\mathrm{r/min}$ for 4 min to isolate the solid product. The solid was washed with DMF and EtOH and dried at $60^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ overnight to obtain the green powder.
Synthesis of MIL-101-HOPO. MIL-101- $\cdot N H_{2}$ $(46m g),$ 3,2-HOPO-COOH $\{30~\mathsf{m g}\}$ , and EDC·HCl $(35~\mathsf{m g})$ were added into DMF $(7~\mathsf{m L})$ with a molar ratio of 1 : 3 : 3. The mixture was heated at $50~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $6\mathrm{~h~}$ in an oven, and was then centrifuged at 11 000 $\mathsf{r/m i n}$ for 4 min to isolate the solid product. The product was washed repeatedly with methanol, and dried at $60^{\circ}\mathsf C$ overnight to obtain the final green powder.
## Characterization
PXRD data were collected from $3^{\circ}$ to $30^{\circ}$ at $40~\mathsf{k V}$ and $40~\mathsf{m A}$ with Cu Kα radiation $(\lambda=0.154056\ \mathrm{nm})$ ) and a Lynxeye one-dimensional detector with a step of 0.03. FT-IR spectroscopy was performed using a Thermo Nicolet iS50 spectrometer in the range of $4000{-}500c m^{-1}$ . $^1\mathsf{H}$ NMR spectra were collected on an Advance III WB-400 equipment. TEM images were obtained using a FEI Tecnai G2 field emission microscope with an accelerating voltage of $120{\mathsf{k V}}.$ The materials were dispersed into methanol, ultrasonically dispersed and dripped on a copper net to prepare the TEM samples. Data of particle sizes were collected by DLS instrument (Nano-ZS), and the materials were immersed into deionized water and ultrasonically dispersed to prepare the DLS samples. The U(VI) contents were determined by inductively coupled plasma-optical emission spectroscopy (ICP-OES) (Thermo Fisher Scientific iCAP 7000) or inductively coupled plasma-mass spectrometry (ICP-MS) (Thermo Scientific). All of the data were shown as mean ± standard deviation (SD). A two-tailed student's T-test for independent-samples T-test was used to compare the means between two individual groups, $p<0.05$ considered to be statistically significant.
## In vitro adsorption experiments
All the experiments were carried out in open atmosphere and at room temperature. The pH of the reaction system was adjusted by trace amounts of ${\mathsf{H N O}}_{3}/{\mathsf{N a O H}}$ solution.
